>Manners, enforced by peer presure, are what used to keep swear words
>out of conversations.  Maybe if we 'grep'ed all submissions to non-alt
>groups for f*gg*t, f*ck, etc, and rejected anything that flunked
>with a polite note suggesting rephrasing.  And I don't just mean
>user submissions, but anything from other news servers as well.

Problems.  First of all, there are lots of obscene non AngloSaxon words,
and lots 'letter bunches' which make up obscene AS words also make up
totally inoffensive words in other languages.

Second, there are too many alternative meanings, and euphemisms.  'Faggot',
for example, in addition to being slang for 'homosexual' in some cultures,
is also a perfectly good word (in the US as well as the UK) meaning 'a
bundle of sticks', and (in the UK) also refers to a food item which
vaguely resembles a meatball (and, often shows up in soc.culture.british
with that meaning).

I can't think of any 'clean' alternative meanings for 'fuck', but note
that you'll have to get rid, as well, of such words as 'screw' -- which
will wipe out a lot of mechanical topics.  Not to mention 'shaft'
(mechanical again), 'bonk' (a sports jargon word meaning, roughly to
suddenly exhaust your energy supply), ...  

Not to mention, that *I* (me personally) find the use of *'s to 'hide'
these words offensive.  Saying 'f*ck' incorporates not only the word
itself, but also that sort of 'hey, everybody, I just said a bad word,
hee hee' impression that you spot in little kids who've just learned a
new word by watching dad hit his thumb with a hammer.

Your proposed 'AI' cannot be done with grep.  I submit that I could
write an incredibly obscene posting without using any 'naughty' words
at all.  Your 'AI' needs to have solved the same problem as language
translation software needs to solve, and that can't yet be done at a
cost which allows use on any 'normal' machine.  It needs to actually
'understand' what is being said.

Of course, someday it will be solved.  At that point, I submit, it would
still be a bad idea to incorporate it into the news infrastructure.
Better, put it into the news readers (a fine idea) so that if YOU don't
want to see words like that, you can tell your newsreader to protect
you from them.  

(It never ceases to amaze me, the number of people who want someone to
tell them what they are and are not allowed to say.  History indicates
that once you give a group censorship powers, no matter how noble your
reasons for doing so, they will begin to (mis-)use it for their own
benefit.  That's one of the major reasons for the First Amendment to
the US Constitution, and for the US Freedom of Information Act.  The
UK could use something like that.)

