Um, well, I never was a very good parent, but I think I'd rather try to 
explain your first version to a six-year-old than your second. :-)
I refrain from quoting it because if I put in all your disclaimers about
it only being an example then it gets too long, and if I take them out
someone will leap on it out of context.

I really think that the necessary answer is to educate some manners into
people's heads, rather than to try to 'control' things.  I worry about
'control'.  (Who controls the controllers?  Who are they responsible to?
Nevermind the issue of whether it can even be done in this context.)

I'm totally happy, on the other hand, to see control made available at the
user level.  E.g., newsreaders with 'parental control locks' on them so
that you can shield your kids to the degree you see fit.  (I'd suggest
that most kids probably know all the words by the time they are 6 or 7
anyway -- though they probably also know enough not to let their parents
know that they know them -- so that you're probably better off having them
there so you can explain why 'polite people' don't (normally) use them.)

I see parallels between news and Unix itself, though.  Unix was originally
developed to be used by (relatively small) groups of responsible people
who knew each other and who could be expected to cooperate with each other.
Now it's spread into the bigger world, which isn't like that, and various
things have been tacked on to allow people to protect themselves from
hostile 'outsiders'.  Despite this, people still break in.

News is having similar evolutionary problems.  Some things probably
need to be either secured or abandoned -- e.g. it would be nice if
sendsys could be locked up.  However, all in all, I would feel the need
to argue against censoring at the posting and distribution level -- even
though I know who'd be first to go if I had the power, which is also why
I don't think it's a good idea to give anyone the power.  

If 'protection' against articles is required by certain people, I'd
rather see them given the power to voluntarily elect it at the reader
level.  (This is in contrast to 'protection of the whole system against
attack', which I think IS a reasonable goal and which is why I've got
it in for sendsys.)

Much like cable TV.  If you want to cut the cables, I'd have to argue
with you.  If you want a 'parental lock' on your cable decoder so your
kids can't watch the adult movie channel, that's your business.

