   At this time it is not clear where the right to free speech conflicts
   with the right to enjoy a harassment-free workplace. 

One useful delineation is that free speech covers the right to express
ideas and the right to express them at a time and place to those who
are willing to listen to you, but does not include the right to
_force_ others to listen to you.  The principle is easy to state 
but the _precise_ boundary line is impossible to draw.  However,
reminding people that there are limits can often be useful.

Historical note: One of the most famous instances of this was the
turmoil in the ACLU over whether a planned march by Nazis in Cicero
Illinois (in the late 1970's) represented excercise of their "free
speech" rights.  The ACLU decided that it did, but there were many
members who disagreed (holding that the ACLU was not supporting free
speech, but the abuse of it) and left the organization.

