>>"The Federal Department of Education has ruled that master's and
>>doctoral theses--research papers normally bound and put on the shelves
>>at schools nationwide--are student 'educational records,' much like
>>grade reports.  That means that they can not be checked out of
>>libraries, sent to faraway researchers, or called up through computer
>>databases without the author's permission, the News & Observer of
>>Raleigh (N.C.) reported."
>>
>>The article goes on to say that making theses and dissertations
>>available for public use without the author's permission is a violation
>>of the Federal Education Rights and Privacy Act.  

It seems to be that we've gone just a bit overboard with FERPA in
this instance.  I believe that theses/dissertations are, by their
very nature, public record; while it is true that some such docu-
ments may not be freely distributed (some folks do Master's and
Ph.D. work in areas of national security, and their dissertations
are often classified), the vast majority of them reflect both the
instructional and physical support of the University.  The notion
that the University should provide lab facilities, instructors,
other resources, and money to the MA/MS/Ph.D student *without*
having the ability to distribute the end result of the research 
is just a bit farfetched for my taste.

The implications of this ruling on literature searches by future
MS/MA/Ph.D. students seem rather extreme.  How can a student select
a "new" topic without access to previous research?

>>Ways to comply with
>>the law include having current students sign a waiver, tracking down
>>former students to get permission, or taking authors' names off theses
>>(this last has interesting implications for cataloging!).

Out of idle curiosity, how would you handle a case in which the author
has died?

--Wes

